<edge source="iran_gulf_offensive::maritime_accessibility" target="ai_tech_power_shift::model_deployment_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Reduced maritime route accessibility delays arrival of GPUs, networking hardware, and data‑center components, directly slowing the pace at which trained models are integrated into production environments.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_offensive</data>
  <data key="d40">ai_tech_power_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_offensive::maritime_accessibility" target="global_monetary_tighteni::market_liquidity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Improved maritime accessibility lowers trade/frictional risks and commodity/FX volatility and insurance costs, encouraging market‑makers to maintain tighter spreads and deeper books, whereas disruptions cause market‑making pullback and wider bid‑ask spreads.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_offensive</data>
  <data key="d40">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_offensive::maritime_accessibility" target="russia_west_confrontatio::trade_disruption_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Reduced accessibility of key Gulf sea lanes (e.g., Strait of Hormuz) immediately raises shipping delays, insurance and rerouting costs, directly disrupting trade flows between Western markets and external partners and increasing overall trade disruption in the Russia–West context.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_offensive</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_west_confrontatio</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_offensive::maritime_accessibility" target="us_china_strategic_rival::security_tension" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained threats to key sea lanes force both the U.S. and China to assert naval protection missions and convoy policies, increasing the chance of rival naval interactions and elevating bilateral strategic tension.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_offensive</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
</edge>
