<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::policy_rate_level" target="ai_tech_power_shift::ai_investment_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher policy rates raise the cost of debt and increase discount rates used by VCs and corporates, reducing fundraising, valuations and capital available for AI startups and infrastructure, thereby shrinking AI investment flows.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d40">ai_tech_power_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::policy_rate_level" target="iran_gulf_offensive::energy_shipment_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising policy rates increase short-term borrowing and trade‑finance costs for oil traders and ship operators (higher LC and working‑capital prices), prompting shipment deferments, cancellations and lower cargo throughput through Gulf routes.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_gulf_offensive</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::policy_rate_level" target="us_china_strategic_rival::policy_uncertainty" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained higher policy rates worsen macroeconomic strain and fiscal pressure, prompting ad‑hoc regulatory interventions, emergency industrial measures and frequent rule changes that raise firms' perceived policy unpredictability.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
</edge>
