</edge>
<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::investor_risk_appetite" target="ai_tech_power_shift::model_deployment_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">An increase in investor risk appetite expands venture and growth funding and willingness to back unproven product launches, enabling AI firms and startups to accelerate rolling models into production and commercial deployments.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d40">ai_tech_power_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::investor_risk_appetite" target="iran_gulf_offensive::conflict_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A drop in investor risk appetite reduces insurance and underwriting capacity for high‑risk Gulf transits and offshore activities, leaving vessels and energy infrastructure more exposed and increasing incentives for opportunistic kinetic attacks and escalation.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_gulf_offensive</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::investor_risk_appetite" target="russia_west_confrontatio::corporate_exposure" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">An increase in investor risk appetite encourages firms and financial sponsors to maintain or expand investments and credit lines in higher‑risk markets (including Russia), directly raising corporate operational and balance‑sheet exposure to the conflict; the reverse holds when appetite falls.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_west_confrontatio</data>
</edge>
