<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::market_liquidity" target="iran_gulf_offensive::logistics_resilience" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A liquidity squeeze causes banks and financiers to pull or reduce trade‑credit, factoring and supplier lines to ports, terminals and carriers, degrading buffer inventories, spare‑parts availability and the ability to reroute or recover from disruptions.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_gulf_offensive</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::market_liquidity" target="russia_west_confrontatio::trade_disruption_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A decline in market liquidity raises trade‑finance and settlement frictions (higher transaction costs, frozen correspondent banking lines), causing immediate cancellations and delays in cross‑border shipments and thus higher trade disruption; conversely improved liquidity eases those frictions and lowers disruptions.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_west_confrontatio</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_monetary_tighteni::market_liquidity" target="us_china_strategic_rival::trade_barrier_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.55</data>
  <data key="d37">A drop in market liquidity raises trade‑finance costs and disrupts payment chains, prompting firms and governments to favor temporary import restrictions, export licensing or local content measures that raise effective trade barriers.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_monetary_tighteni</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
</edge>
